{:edb/db-before-t 2031 :edb/db-after-t 2032 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535344 50 #inst "2026-10-13T21:07:47.246Z" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186054858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500383 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186054862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500292 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062186 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062187 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062188 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062189 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062190 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499922 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499999 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500040 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500065 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500114 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062185 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500040 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186062923 1078 #edb/ref 17592186062928 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500289 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500298 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500317 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500346 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500318 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500381 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500324 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500325 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500334 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500310 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500353 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500382 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064454 1050 900.0 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064688 1071 "![](images/cc/cc27a817900f1e7bef61f73da6bbdb3aaae4e30d59490dce4395416d98f4edae.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $10\\text{ m}$ long?" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064688 1071 "![](images/cc/cc27a817900f1e7bef61f73da6bbdb3aaae4e30d59490dce4395416d98f4edae.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $2m/s$. At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $10m$ long?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186064688 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(2)\\cdot 2(10)=40{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064811 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $10in^{2}/hour$. If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{dx}{dt}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186064811 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $10{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186064811 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\text{ in}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{5}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186065380 1064 "Suppose that the lengths of the sides of a square are growing at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e791c7c33636ef3b2b798fde58825cf4cf1f76e3b6abdd2d92317aa555e1420.png)\n\nIf the square is growing, then its area is also increasing. Our question is, \"*what is the rate at which the area of the square is increasing*?\" In other words, if $A$ is the area of the square at time $t$, then what is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the length of the side of the square. We know that the relationship between the area $A$ and side length $x$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $x$ grows at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm/s},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2 \\cdot 2x =4x.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it. We're done!\n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $x$ at that moment into the above.\n\nFor example, to work out the rate of change of area when $t=2$ seconds, we substitute the corresponding value of $x$ into the formula. When $t=2$ we have $x=4$ (from the diagram) and therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4(4) = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186065380 1064 "Suppose that the lengths of the sides of a square are growing at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e791c7c33636ef3b2b798fde58825cf4cf1f76e3b6abdd2d92317aa555e1420.png)\n\nIf the square is growing, then its area is also increasing. Our question is, \"*what is the rate at which the area of the square is increasing*?\" In other words, if $A$ is the area of the square at time $t$, then what is\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}?$$\n\nLet $x$ be the length of the side of the square. We know that the relationship between the area $A$ and side length $x$ is given by\n\n$$A = x^2.$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe're told that $x$ grows at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm/s},$ so we have\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = 2.$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2 \\cdot 2x =4x.$$\n\nAnd that's it. We're done!\n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $x$ at that moment into the above.\n\nFor example, to work out the rate of change of area when $t=2$ seconds, we substitute the corresponding value of $x$ into the formula. When $t=2$ we have $x=4$ (from the diagram) and therefore\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4(4) = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186065382 1064 "Suppose that the rate of change of the diagonal of a square is known, and we're interested in finding the rate of change of some other quantity. For example, can we calculate the rate of change of the area of a square given the rate of change of its diagonal?\n\nBefore we get into that, let's recall a few facts about squares. Consider the square $ABCD$ with sides of length $x$ and diagonals of length $h.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cc3f0cd81cae3bab92ec6c3826f64b7e54e0af61cc32a000569afe5171a27dc.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the area $A$ of a square is given by $A = x^2.$ So, since $x^2 = \\dfrac{h^2}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis formula will prove particularly handy in the next example." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186065382 1064 "Suppose that the rate of change of the diagonal of a square is known, and we're interested in finding the rate of change of some other quantity. For example, can we calculate the rate of change of the area of a square given the rate of change of its diagonal?\n\nBefore we get into that, let's recall a few facts about squares. Consider the square $ABCD$ with sides of length $x$ and diagonals of length $h.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cc3f0cd81cae3bab92ec6c3826f64b7e54e0af61cc32a000569afe5171a27dc.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe also know that the area $A$ of a square is given by $A = x^2.$ So, since $x^2 = \\dfrac{h^2}{2},$ we have\n\n$$A = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.$$\n\nThis formula will prove particularly handy in the next example." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073071 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073072 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073073 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073074 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073075 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499883 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499951 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500030 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500045 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500054 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073070 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499951 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073106 1078 #edb/ref 17592186073109 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073137 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073138 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073139 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073140 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073141 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499967 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500093 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500105 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500155 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073136 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500160 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073136 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499967 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073142 1078 #edb/ref 17592186073145 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073567 1074 "The tape diagram for big tokens shows $4$ boxes. So, there are $4$ big tokens.\n\nAltogether, there are\n\n$$\n12+4=16\n$$\n\nboxes. So, there are $16$ tokens in all.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of big tokens to all tokens is $4:16$." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073567 1074 "The tape diagram for big tokens shows $4$ boxes. So, there are $4$ big tokens.\n\nAltogether, there are $12+4=16$ boxes. So, there are $16$ tokens in all.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of big tokens to all tokens is $4:16.$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073569 1074 "We are told that:\n\n> Abbie makes $\\color{blue}5$ cupcakes for every $\\color{red}7$ that Kate makes.\n\nSo, in a tape diagram, Abbie's tape must contain $\\color{blue}5$ boxes, while Kate's tape should have $\\color{red}7$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/47/47c6e186cd17bffc7e83cca0c228bc1d05287cfba1bdb394c0a5dd71d411294a.png)" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073569 1074 "We are told that:\n\nAbbie makes $\\color{blue}5$ cupcakes for every $\\color{red}7$ that Kate makes.\n\nSo, in a tape diagram, Abbie's tape must contain $\\color{blue}5$ boxes, while Kate's tape should have $\\color{red}7$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/47/47c6e186cd17bffc7e83cca0c228bc1d05287cfba1bdb394c0a5dd71d411294a.png)" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073574 1074 "We are told that there are $54$ orange plastic building blocks. The tape diagram for orange plastic building blocks shows $6$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents\n\n$$\n54 \\div 6 = 9\n$$\n\nplastic building blocks.\n\nNow, we fill the number $9$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/16c543fb48a3d3c89c36b969cd4fceec36dd0c880664fd979c76740fd12c5722.png)\n\nSince there are $10$ boxes corresponding to the plastic building blocks in total, and each box represents $9$ plastic building blocks, the total number of plastic building blocks is:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n$$\n\nSo, there are $90$ plastic building blocks." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073574 1074 "We are told that there are $54$ orange plastic building blocks. The tape diagram for orange plastic building blocks shows $6$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $54 \\div 6 = 9$ plastic building blocks.\n\nNow, we fill the number $9$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/16c543fb48a3d3c89c36b969cd4fceec36dd0c880664fd979c76740fd12c5722.png)\n\nSince there are $10$ boxes corresponding to the plastic building blocks in total, and each box represents $9$ plastic building blocks, the total number of plastic building blocks is:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n$$\n\nSo, there are $90$ plastic building blocks." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073575 1074 "The tape diagram shows $1$ box corresponding to winning tickets, and $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets. Therefore, the ratio of winning to nonwinning tickets is $1:4$.\n\nWe are told that there are $60$ tickets in total. The tape diagram has\n\n$$\n1+4=5\n$$\n\nboxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents\n\n$$\n60 \\div 5 = 12\n$$\n\ntickets.\n\nNow, we fill the number $12$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/1669ac0d1aa6edd5bf53609121080dc8e8b685b57daa60b91daf3db816474ef2.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets, and each box represents $12$ tickets, the number of nonwinning tickets must be:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 12 = 48\n$$\n\nSo, there are $48$ nonwinning tickets in the jar." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186073575 1074 "The tape diagram shows $1$ box corresponding to winning tickets, and $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets. Therefore, the ratio of winning to nonwinning tickets is $1:4.$\n\nWe are told that there are $60$ tickets in total. The tape diagram has $1+4=5$ boxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $60 \\div 5 = 12$ tickets.\n\nNow, we fill the number $12$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/1669ac0d1aa6edd5bf53609121080dc8e8b685b57daa60b91daf3db816474ef2.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets, and each box represents $12$ tickets, the number of nonwinning tickets must be:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 12 = 48.\n$$\n\nSo, there are $48$ nonwinning tickets in the jar." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500333 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500288 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500285 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500294 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500356 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500284 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500287 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500295 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500358 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500379 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500297 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500313 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500354 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500370 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500282 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500291 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500319 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500374 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186073974 1050 900.0 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074472 1074 "The tape diagram for incorrect answers shows $2$ boxes while the tape for correct answers contains $5$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of incorrect answers to correct answers is $2:5.$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074478 1074 "We are told that there are $45$ stairs in the warehouse. The tape diagram for the stairs shows $5$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $45÷5=9.$\n\nNow, we fill the number $9$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/db/db352d53c2926e7a42b8183088a35860f057f4e5a845554ada955303e3dc8926.png)\n\nSince there are $9$ boxes corresponding to the stairs and carts in total, and each box represents $9$ of these items, the total number of stairs and carts are:\n\n$$\n9\\times 9=81\n$$\n\nSo, there are $81$ stairs and carts altogether." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074483 1074 "The tape diagram for cheap soaps shows $6$ boxes while the tape diagram has $6+3=9$ boxes in total.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of cheap soaps to all soaps is $6:9.$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074484 1071 "![](images/8d/8d861b8d473ffe6b56105636da5ea51bb5fd33685b454800d376616b5cf2e311.png)\n\nThe password of Carla's computer has ten characters. Each character is a number among $1$ and $9$. The ratio of odd numbers to even numbers is shown in the diagram above. How many even numbers her password has?" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186074484 1071 "![](images/8d/8d861b8d473ffe6b56105636da5ea51bb5fd33685b454800d376616b5cf2e311.png)\n\nThe password of Carla's computer has ten characters. Each character is a number among $1$ and $9.$ The ratio of odd numbers to even numbers is shown in the diagram above. How many even numbers her password has?" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186074484 1074 "The tape diagram shows $2$ boxes corresponding to odd numbers, and $3$ boxes corresponding to even numbers. Therefore, the ratio of odd to even in the password is $2:3.$\n\nWe are told that the password has $10$ characters. The tape diagram has $2+3=5$ boxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $10÷5=2$ numbers.\n\nNow, we fill the number $2$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc4dedfb2550e65ad1146b5103fc00ac1cc0d447258d3366