e than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents\n\n$$\n15 \\div 3 = 5\n$$\n\nunits.\n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186074971 1064 "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $15 \\div 3 = 5$ units. \n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186104956 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104961 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105438 1071 "Given the pentagon $ABCDE$ shown below, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/dc/dc4407245e0639d9055d9518088a46b310f67e897337bd8963e5b1c42a86421f.png)" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186105438 1071 "Given the pentagon $ABCDE$ shown below, find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc4407245e0639d9055d9518088a46b310f67e897337bd8963e5b1c42a86421f.png)" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105438 1074 "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (5 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 540^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D + m∠E &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n120^{∘} + 110^{∘} + 2x + 90^{∘} + x + 40^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n2x + x + 40^{∘} + 120^{∘} + 110^{∘} + 90^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 360^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186105438 1074 "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {120}^{∘}+{110}^{∘}+2x+{90}^{∘}+x+{40}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+x+{40}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}+{110}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{360}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105439 1071 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is $720^\\circ$. How many sides does it have?" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186105439 1071 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is $720^\\circ.$ How many sides does it have?" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105439 1074 "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2) = 720^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 720^{∘} \\\\\nn - 2 &= \\frac{720^{∘}}{180^{∘}} \\\\\nn - 2 &= 4 \\\\\nn &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=6$ sides (it is a hexagon)." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186105439 1074 "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2) = 720^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={720}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{{720}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =4 \\\\ n & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=6$ sides (it is a hexagon)." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105440 1071 "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\n1. $40^{\\circ}$, $50^{\\circ}$, $100^{\\circ}$, $170^{\\circ}$\n2. $20^{\\circ}$, $50^{\\circ}$, $150^{\\circ}$, $140^{\\circ}$\n3. $30^{\\circ}$, $35^{\\circ}$, $140^{\\circ}$, $175^{\\circ}$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186105440 1071 "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\nI. $40^{\\circ}$, $50^{\\circ}$, $100^{\\circ}$, $170^{\\circ}$\n\nII. $20^{\\circ}$, $50^{\\circ}$, $150^{\\circ}$, $140^{\\circ}$\n\nIII. $30^{\\circ}$, $35^{\\circ}$, $140^{\\circ}$, $175^{\\circ}$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105440 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n40^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 100^{∘} + 170^{∘} = 360^{∘}✓; 20^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 150^{∘} + 140^{∘} = 360^{∘}✓; 30^{∘} + 35^{∘} + 140^{∘} + 175^{∘} = 380^{∘}X\n$$\n\nTherefore, only options I and II could be the angles of a quadrilateral." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186105440 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{40}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{100}^{∘}+{170}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓ \\\\  &  \\\\ {20}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{150}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓ \\\\  &  \\\\ {30}^{∘}+{35}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}+{175}^{∘}={380}^{∘}\\,X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only options I and II could be the angles of a quadrilateral." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500366 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500330 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500342 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500365 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500375 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500302 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500305 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500348 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186105973 1050 660.0 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186106468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186106468 1071 "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\n1. $64^{∘}$, $73^{∘}$, $98^{∘}$, $124^{∘}$\n2. $73^{∘}$, $112^{∘}$, $93^{∘}$, $82^{∘}$\n3. $80^{∘}$, $140^{∘}$, $49^{∘}$, $84^{∘}$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186106468 1071 "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\nI. ${64}^{∘}$, ${73}^{∘}$, ${98}^{∘}$, ${124}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${73}^{∘}$, ${112}^{∘}$, ${93}^{∘}$, ${82}^{∘}$\n\nIII. ${80}^{∘}$, ${140}^{∘}$, ${49}^{∘}$, ${84}^{∘}$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186106468 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{64}^{∘}+{73}^{∘}+{98}^{∘}+{124}^{∘}={359}^{∘}\\,X \\\\  &  \\\\ {73}^{∘}+{112}^{∘}+{93}^{∘}+{82}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓ \\\\  &  \\\\ {80}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}+{49}^{∘}+{84}^{∘}={353}^{∘}\\,X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only option II could be the angles of a quadrilateral." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186107137 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n> *The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (3 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 180^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ$, which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186107137 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute $n={\\color{blue}3}$ into the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ,$ which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186107138 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nsides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186107138 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has $n={\\color{blue}4}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186107139 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nsides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (5 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 540^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186107139 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has $n={\\color{blue}5}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186107170 1064 "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson.\n\n> *The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon.\n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral**<br><br><br>Number of vertices: $4$<br><br><br>Number of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$<br><br><br>Sum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon**<br><br><br>Number of vertices: $5$<br><br><br>Number of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$<br><br><br>Sum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon**<br><br><br>Number of vertices: $6$<br><br><br>Number of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$<br><br><br>Sum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles.\n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ$, and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2)\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186107170 1064 "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum o