f 17592186126788 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126789 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126790 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499673 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499696 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499780 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499832 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186126785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500137 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186126785 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499673 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127640 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127641 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127642 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127643 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127644 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499836 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500063 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500080 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500135 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186127639 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500140 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186127639 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500135 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500372 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500327 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500335 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129548 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129548 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $12kg$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 2}$,\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2m$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186129548 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{0+2}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6(x-1)^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2(x-1)^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4-(-2) \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =8+{2}^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =8+48 \\\\  & =56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129693 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186129693 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1m$ with mass density $λ(x, y) = \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$, measured in $kg/m^{2}$, about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186129693 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ its center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}=r.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{15}+\\frac{10\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186133728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500304 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186133729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500293 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186133848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500322 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186138368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500332 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186147415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500376 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186147474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500299 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186147474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500378 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186147475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500311 1319