}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1-3x-3y}{x+y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176220 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-3(x+y)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1-3z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1-2z}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1-2z}{z}.$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176332 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in centimeters per minute, we divide the number of centimeters, which is $90,$ by the number of minutes, $20.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{90}{20} & =\\frac{90÷10}{20÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9÷2 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the slug is $4.5$ centimeters per minute. \n\nTo find the time it took the slug to cover $450$ centimeters, we divide the distance by $4.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{450}{4.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{450\\cdot 10}{4.5\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,500}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{45\\cdot 100}{45} \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the slug $100$ minutes to crawl $450$ centimeters." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176463 1071 "If $-3a^{2} + 30a - 72 =-3(a + p)(a + q)$, then $p + q =$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186176463 1071 "If $-3{a}^{2}+30a-72=-3(a+p)(a+q),$ then $p+q=$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176463 1074 "First, notice that we can factor out a $-3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}^{2}+30a-72 & =-3({a}^{2}-10a+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${a}^{2}-10a+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-24) \\\\ (-2) & \\times (-12) \\\\ (-3) & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-4) & \\times (-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-24) & =-25 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-12) & =-14 & \\times  \\\\ (-3)+(-8) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-4)+(-6) & =-10 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-10a=-4a-6a,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({a}^{2}-10a+24) & = \\\\ -3({a}^{2}-4a-6a+24) & = \\\\ -3(a(a-4)-6(a-4)) & = \\\\ -3(a-4)(a-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,q=-6,$ and $p+q=-10.$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175558 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186176961 1064 "When an object moves at a constant speed $v$, the distance $d$ that it travels and the time $t$ are related by the equation\n\n$$\nd = vt\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to know how long it takes for an object to travel a *fixed* distance. We start by rewriting the above equation for $t\\mathbin$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= \\frac{d}{v}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $d$ is fixed (i.e. constant), it follows that the time $t$ varies inversely with the velocity $v$, and $d$ is the constant of variation.\n\nIntuitively, the fact that this is an inverse variation makes sense. The larger the speed of our object, the smaller the time it takes to cover a fixed distance, and vice-versa.\n\nSuppose we're told that an object takes $t=10$ seconds to cover a particular distance when traveling with a speed of $v=5$ meters per second. Then, we can solve for $d$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= \\frac{d}{v} \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{d}{5} \\\\\n10 \\cdot 5 &= d \\\\\n50 &= d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the inverse variation equation\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{50}{v}\n$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to find the amount of time the object takes to cover the same fixed distance for different speeds." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186176961 1064 "When an object moves at a constant speed $v,$ the distance $d$ that it travels and the time $t$ are related by the equation\n\n$$\nd = vt.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to know how long it takes for an object to travel a *fixed* distance. We start by rewriting the above equation for $t\\mathbin :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{d}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $d$ is fixed (i.e. constant), it follows that the time $t$ varies inversely with the velocity $v,$ and $d$ is the constant of variation. \n\nIntuitively, the fact that this is an inverse variation makes sense. The larger the speed of our object, the smaller the time it takes to cover a fixed distance, and vice-versa.\n\nSuppose we're told that an object takes $t=10$ seconds to cover a particular distance when traveling with a speed of $v=5$ meters per second. Then, we can solve for $d$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{d}{v} \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{d}{5} \\\\ 10\\cdot 5 & =d \\\\ 50 & =d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the inverse variation equation\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{50}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to find the amount of time the object takes to cover the same fixed distance for different speeds." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186176965 1064 "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial\n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large!\n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8$. If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 8x^2 + 24x + 16 \\\\[5pt] &= 8(x^2 + 3x + 2) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2)\n$$\n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient\n\n$$\n(1+2=3)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the linear term as\n\n$$\n3x=x+2x\n$$\n\nand factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3x + 2 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + x + 2x + 2 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + x) + (2x + 2) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 1) + 2(x + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 2)(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x^{2} + 24x + 16 &=  \\\\\n8(x^{2} + 3x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= 8(x + 2)(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186176965 1064 "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial \n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large! \n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8.$ If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t8x^2 + 24x + 16 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t8(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient $(1+2=3).$ \n\nSo, we can write the linear term as $3x=x+2x$ and factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+2 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+2x+2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x)+(2x+2) & = \\\\ x(x+1)+2(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+2)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}+24x+16 & = \\\\ 8({x}^{2}+3x+2) & = \\\\ 8(x+2)(x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186177027 1064 "![](images/a2/a2bbedb8a024b6cd75c333e2c0cf025558b71eafeb7215d9c7dbf2fbd678230a.png)\n\nIt is known that the gravitational force $F$, measured in newtons, that the earth exerts on the moon varies inversely with the **square** of the distance $r$, in kilometers, between their centers.\n\nAccording to this information, the equation that relates $F$ and $r$ is\n\n$$\nF = \\dfrac{k}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of variation. This type of relationship is called an **inverse square law**.\n\nAs it turns out, many important physical laws take the form of an inverse square law. Let's see another example." 13194139535344 17592186045417 false] [17592186177027 1064 "![](images/a2/a2bbedb8a024b6cd75c333e2c0cf025558b71eafeb7215d9c7dbf2fbd678230a.png)\n\nIt is known that the gravitational force $F,$ measured in newtons, that the earth exerts on the moon varies inversely with the **square** of the distance $r,$ in kilometers, between their centers.\n\nAccording to this information, the equation that relates $F$ and $r$ is\n\n$$\nF = \\dfrac{k}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of variation. This type of relationship is called an **inverse square law**.\n\nAs it turns out, many important physical laws take the form of an inverse square law. Let's see another example." 13194139535344 17592186045417 true] [17592186198259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500321 13194139535344 17592186